---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../config';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="HAP - Human authorization for consequential AI actions"
  description="HAP is the open, MIT-licensed protocol for bounded AI-agent authority. Humans authorize; an agent executes only once a signed receipt has been issued for the action."
>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       1. HERO
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="hero-body">
        <p class="label">Open Protocol · v{siteConfig.version}</p>
        {/* Same words as siteConfig.tagline, which the spec pages render as their
            brand line. Marked up by hand here for the accent span, so the two
            copies must be changed together. */}
        <h1>Give AI autonomy <br class="desktop-break">without giving it <span class="hl">authority</span>.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          A human sets what the automation may do — scope, limits, duration. Every consequential
          action is checked against that mandate before it runs, and produces a signed receipt
          proving the authorization existed first. Not a log written afterwards. A precondition.
        </p>
        <div class="cta-group">
          <a href="/protocol" class="btn btn-primary">Read the Protocol</a>
          <button type="button" id="copy-spec" class="btn btn-secondary" data-src="/context.txt">Copy for your AI</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Names what is below rather than saying "scroll": the reader learns the
           next section, and the link works without JS or a pointing device. -->
      <a class="scroll-cue" href="#invariant">
        <span class="scroll-cue-label">See how it works</span>
        <span class="scroll-cue-track" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       3. NO RECEIPT. NO EXECUTION.
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec" id="invariant">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">The core invariant</p>
      <h2>No receipt. No execution.</h2>
      <p class="body-lead">
        The receipt is not a post-execution log. It is the precondition for execution —
        and, afterwards, the verifiable proof that authorization existed before it ran.
        Agents request execution; they do not hold the keys.
      </p>

      <div class="invariant-cols">
        <div class="receipt" role="figure" aria-label="Execution receipt example">
          <div class="receipt-head">
            Execution receipt
            <span class="receipt-tag">signed · pre-execution</span>
          </div>
          <dl class="receipt-fields">
            <dt class="rf-key">profileId</dt>
            <dd class="rf-val">charge@0.4</dd>
            <dt class="rf-key">actionType</dt>
            <dd class="rf-val">refund</dd>
            <dt class="rf-key">boundsHash</dt>
            <dd class="rf-val">sha256:7a91… <span class="rf-note">— the mandate this ran under</span></dd>
            <dt class="rf-key">executionContext</dt>
            <dd class="rf-val">order #4821 · €48.00</dd>
            <dt class="rf-key">cumulativeState</dt>
            <dd class="rf-val">€391 / €500 today <span class="rf-note">— enforced by the Authority Server</span></dd>
            <dt class="rf-key">subjects</dt>
            <dd class="rf-val">did:key:z6Mk… <span class="rf-note">— authorizing human, disclosed opt-in</span></dd>
            <dt class="rf-key">timestamp</dt>
            <dd class="rf-val">2026-05-23T14:22Z</dd>
            <dt class="rf-key">signature</dt>
            <dd class="rf-val">ed25519:8f3b…1b4c</dd>
          </dl>
        </div>

        <ol class="chain">
          <li>A human authorizes <strong>bounded execution</strong> — scope, limits, time, and commitment mode.</li>
          <li>The <span class="role">Gatekeeper</span> verifies the attestation, checks per-action bounds, and enforces local context constraints.</li>
          <li>The <span class="role">Authority Server</span> checks cumulative limits, expiry, and revocation, then <strong>signs the receipt</strong>.</li>
          <li>The <span class="role">Executor</span> runs the action — only <strong>after</strong> the receipt exists.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <p class="neutrality">
        <strong>Open infrastructure.</strong> Any compliant Authority Server can
        issue receipts — Suveren is one implementation. The protocol is open;
        no single vendor owns the trust layer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       3b. ONE SIGNED ACT — THREE JOBS
       The three columns state what holds TODAY (the Authority Server vouches).
       The "what comes next" box is deliberate: co-signature is specified and
       unbuilt, and naming that is more credible than implying it already ships.
       Keep it in step with content/<version>/review.md — if the register moves,
       this box moves.
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec sec-alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">One signed act</p>
      <h2>One receipt. Three jobs.</h2>
      <p class="body-lead">
        Most systems make you choose: a gate that blocks, or a log that explains.
        The same signed artifact does both, and a third thing neither can.
      </p>

      <div class="jobs">
        <div>
          <div class="job-name"><span class="dir">Now</span>It runs, or it doesn't.</div>
          <p>Every consequential action is checked against the mandate before it happens.
            Not caught in a log afterwards. Prevented.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="job-name"><span class="dir">Outward</span>A stranger can check it.</div>
          <p>Hand someone the receipt and they verify the signature and the content
            themselves — no account, no relationship with you. Cryptography, not testimony.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="job-name"><span class="dir">Back</span>You can answer for it.</div>
          <p>Evidence created before the act, not reconstructed after: which mandate,
            which bounds, which content. What an auditor, an insurer, or a court asks for.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="today">
        <p class="label">What holds today, and what comes next</p>
        <p>
          Today the Authority Server signs that the human authorized it — the chain holds
          as far as you trust one operator's key. Next, the human's own key co-signs the
          mandate. Then it holds <strong>without trusting any operator, including us</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="soul">
        The same receipt does all three. That is what a signed artifact can do
        and a permission cannot.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       4. WHERE IT FITS
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">Where it fits</p>
      <h2>HAP composes. It doesn't compete.</h2>
      <p class="body-lead">
        HAP isn't another login, API gateway, agent framework, sandbox, or policy engine.
        It's the layer that decides whether an already-reachable capability may be
        used for a <strong>consequential action</strong> — with proof.
      </p>

      <!-- Vendor names are illustrative and deliberately singular per row: the
           category carries the recognition, the example only makes it concrete.
           These products rename faster than anything else in the table — if one
           is stale, drop the parenthetical rather than chasing the rename. -->
      <table class="contrast">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="old">OAuth / OpenID Connect</td><td class="old">Grants API access</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="old">Identity (EUDI, passkeys, WebAuthn)</td><td class="old">Proves who you are</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="old">MCP</td><td class="old">Exposes tools to agents</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="old">Agent identity and lifecycle <span class="eg">(e.g. Entra Agent ID)</span></td><td class="old">Governs which agents exist and what they may reach</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="old">Agent platforms and policy engines <span class="eg">(e.g. Bedrock AgentCore)</span></td><td class="old">Run agents and evaluate configured rules per call</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="hap">HAP</td><td class="hap">Authorizes the consequential execution — against a mandate a named human committed to, before it runs, with a signed receipt</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p class="compose-note">
        Every other layer here answers <em>can this system reach that?</em> — an access
        question, and each answers it well. None of them holds a bounded mandate a named
        human committed to, and none produces an artifact a third party can verify for
        themselves. HAP adds that layer and replaces none of them: keep your identity
        provider, your agent platform, your policy engine, your sandbox and secrets
        manager. Point them at the agent path, and put the mandate above.
      </p>

      <p class="couplet"><span class="a">OAuth grants reachable capability.</span> <span class="b">HAP governs authorized use of that capability.</span></p>
      <p class="couplet"><span class="a">Agent platforms decide what an agent may reach.</span> <span class="b">HAP records what a human actually mandated — and proves this act was inside it.</span></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       5. COMMITMENT MODES
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec sec-alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">Commitment modes</p>
      <h2>You decide how much runs on its own.</h2>
      <p class="body-lead">
        The level of autonomy is a signed choice on every authorization — the
        protocol's <strong>commitment mode</strong>, not a default the agent can change.
      </p>

      <div class="modes">
        <div>
          <div class="mode-name">Automatic<span class="who">Agent runs within bounds</span></div>
          <p>The agent acts within the bounds you set. No per-action approval — the
            Authority Server enforces the limits and issues a signed receipt for each
            action before execution.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mode-name">Review<span class="who">You approve each action</span></div>
          <p>The agent proposes; you approve each action before it runs.
            No approval, no receipt — no execution.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="mode-name">Review above a cap<span class="who">Approvers sign above a limit</span></div>
          <p>The agent runs on its own under a limit you set. Above it, the action
            routes to a named set of approvers before any receipt is issued.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       6. THE DISTINCTION
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">The distinction</p>
      <h2>Agents aren't employees. They're executors.</h2>
      <p class="body-lead">
        Most systems give agents accounts, credentials, and standing
        permissions — turning them into authority-bearing actors. HAP does the opposite.
      </p>
      <p>
        An agent never carries authority or raw credentials of its own. A human authorizes a bounded
        action; the <strong>receipt</strong> is cryptographic proof that this specific
        action was authorized — and issued before it ran. An agent may have technical
        identifiers for logging and routing, but it holds no <strong>independent authority</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="soul">
        As AI systems become more capable, HAP keeps authority from quietly
        moving from humans into machines.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       7. TWO MODELS FOR AI AUTHORITY
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec sec-alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">The model choice</p>
      <h2>Two models for authorizing agents.</h2>
      <p class="body-lead">
        How a system treats agent authority is an architectural decision with
        long-term governance consequences.
      </p>

      <table class="contrast">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Agent-identity approach</th>
            <th>HAP approach</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="old">Treat agents as authority-bearing actors</td>
            <td class="hap">Treat agents as executors</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="old">Manage standing agent permissions</td>
            <td class="hap">Issue bounded action receipts</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="old">Hand agents standing credentials</td>
            <td class="hap">Keep credentials behind the execution boundary</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="old">Audit after execution</td>
            <td class="hap">Verify the receipt before execution</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="old">Agent acts under standing authority</td>
            <td class="hap">Agent acts only inside receipt-approved bounds</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="old">Post-incident, the answer is “the agent was permitted”</td>
            <td class="hap">Post-incident, the answer is “this named person mandated this scope — here is the proof”</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="a14">
        <p class="label">EU AI Act — Article 14</p>
        <p>
          Article 14 requires effective human oversight of high-risk AI. HAP turns
          that oversight into an <strong>enforceable control at the action layer</strong>:
          consequential actions cannot run unless a human-linked authorization receipt
          exists before execution.
        </p>
        <p class="general">
          Wherever an organization must show a human stood behind an automated act —
          regulation, audit, insurance, or litigation — the receipt is the artifact.
        </p>
        <p class="caveat">
          HAP is Article-14-enabling infrastructure — not compliance on its own.
          Compliance still requires governance, training, documentation, and human
          competence.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       7b. FOR WHOM
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">For whom</p>
      <h2>Three readers, one artifact.</h2>
      <div class="audience-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="who">Engineering</div>
          <p>Deploy agents on the infrastructure you already have. One enforcement point,
            no new stack, whichever model you run.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="who">Compliance and audit</div>
          <p>The same gate produces the evidence: who mandated it, what it covered,
            proven before it ran.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="who">Legal and counterparties</div>
          <p>Proof that survives leaving your organization — verifiable without trusting
            the operator.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       8. USE CASES
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec sec-alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">Use cases</p>
      <h2>Where consequential actions need proof.</h2>
      <div class="usecases">
        <div>Payments<span>refunds, charges, payouts</span></div>
        <div>Email<span>high-stakes sends</span></div>
        <div>CRM<span>record changes &amp; deletes</span></div>
        <div>Infrastructure<span>deploys &amp; config</span></div>
        <div>Multi-owner approvals<span>two people must sign</span></div>
        <div>Compliance audit<span>verifiable receipts</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       10. OPEN INFRASTRUCTURE
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">Open infrastructure</p>
      <h2>No single vendor owns the trust layer.</h2>
      <p class="body-lead">
        HAP is an open standard — MIT-licensed, developed in the open, and maintained
        by stewards, not owners. Any compliant Authority Server can issue receipts.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-group">
        <a href="/protocol" class="btn btn-primary">Read the Protocol</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/humanagencyprotocol/hap-protocol" class="btn btn-secondary">View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>

<script>
  // Copy the full protocol context (/context.txt) to the clipboard for pasting into an AI chat.
  const btn = document.getElementById('copy-spec') as HTMLButtonElement | null;
  if (btn) {
    const original = btn.textContent;
    btn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      try {
        const res = await fetch(btn.dataset.src || '/context.txt');
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(await res.text());
        btn.textContent = 'Copied — paste into your AI';
        btn.disabled = true;
        setTimeout(() => { btn.textContent = original; btn.disabled = false; }, 2600);
      } catch {
        window.open(btn.dataset.src || '/context.txt', '_blank');
      }
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  #copy-spec { font-family: inherit; }
</style>

<style>
  /* ─── layout wrapper ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .wrap { max-width: 1200px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.5rem; }

  /* ─── hero ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* Near-full viewport. `svh` rather than `vh` so mobile browser chrome does not
     push the scroll cue off-screen and make the section jump on first scroll.
     `min-height`, not `height`: on short viewports the content grows the section
     instead of overflowing it. The nav is position:fixed, so its height is
     reserved as padding rather than subtracted. */
  .hero {
    min-height: calc(100vh - 2rem);
    min-height: calc(100svh - 2rem);
    padding: calc(var(--nav-height) + 2rem) 0 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }
  .hero .wrap { flex: 1; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .hero-body {
    flex: 1;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  /* ─── scroll cue ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* Centred deliberately, against the page's left-aligned rhythm: bottom-centre
     is where a reader already looks for a scroll affordance, and instant
     recognition is worth more here than alignment consistency. The hero content
     above stays left-aligned. */
  .scroll-cue {
    align-self: center;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    padding-bottom: 2.5rem;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .scroll-cue-label {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    /* Accent by default, not on hover — the muted grey was why it went unseen. */
    color: var(--accent);
    transition: color 0.2s;
  }
  .scroll-cue:hover .scroll-cue-label { color: var(--text-primary); }
  .scroll-cue:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 6px; border-radius: 2px; }
  .scroll-cue-track {
    position: relative;
    width: 2px;
    height: 56px;
    background: var(--border);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .scroll-cue-track::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0 auto auto 0;
    width: 2px;
    height: 18px;
    background: var(--accent);
    animation: cue-travel 2.4s cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1) infinite;
  }
  @keyframes cue-travel {
    0%   { transform: translateY(-18px); opacity: 0; }
    25%  { opacity: 1; }
    70%  { opacity: 1; }
    100% { transform: translateY(56px); opacity: 0; }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    /* Parked at the foot of the track — reads as "down" without moving. */
    .scroll-cue-track::after { animation: none; transform: translateY(38px); opacity: 1; }
  }

  /* Short viewports (laptops, landscape phones): the cue costs more than it
     gives once vertical space is tight. */
  @media (max-height: 640px) {
    .hero { min-height: 0; padding: calc(var(--nav-height) + 3rem) 0 4rem; }
    .hero-body { padding-bottom: 0; }
    .scroll-cue { display: none; }
  }

  .hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 5.5vw, 4.2rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.06;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    max-width: 860px;
    margin-bottom: 1.6rem;
  }

  .hero .hl { color: var(--accent); }

  .desktop-break { display: none; }
  @media (min-width: 769px) { .desktop-break { display: block; } }

  .hero .lead {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    max-width: 620px;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .hero .audience {
    margin-top: 1.8rem;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
    max-width: 620px;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* ─── label (eyebrow / section tag) ──────────────────────────────────── */
  .label {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }

  /* ─── couplet ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .couplet {
    margin-top: 2.4rem;
    max-width: 680px;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin-bottom: 2.4rem;
  }
  .couplet .a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  .couplet .b { display: block; color: var(--text-primary); font-weight: 700; }
  /* Two class selectors, so this beats .couplet's 2.4rem top margin. */
  .couplet + .couplet { margin-top: 1.4rem; }

  /* The reach-vs-mandate argument — the load-bearing paragraph of this section. */
  .compose-note {
    margin-top: 2.2rem;
    max-width: 700px;
  }
  .compose-note em { color: var(--text-primary); font-style: italic; }

  /* ─── spec strip ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .spec-strip {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    background: var(--bg-elevated);
  }
  .spec-strip .wrap {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4rem 1rem;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0.8rem 1.5rem;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
  }
  .spec-strip .sep { color: var(--border); user-select: none; }
  .spec-strip strong { color: var(--text-primary); font-weight: 600; }
  .spec-strip a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  .spec-strip a:hover { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }

  /* ─── section layout ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .sec { padding: 6rem 0; }
  .sec + .sec { border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .sec-alt { background: var(--bg-elevated); border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }

  /* ─── section heading pattern ─────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .sec h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3.2vw, 2.6rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
  }
  .sec .body-lead {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    max-width: 680px;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .sec p {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    max-width: 680px;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .sec p strong { color: var(--text-primary); }
  .sec p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  /* ─── invariant / receipt layout ─────────────────────────────────────── */
  .invariant-cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.05fr 0.95fr;
    gap: 3rem;
    align-items: start;
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .invariant-cols { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 2rem; }
  }

  /* receipt — the one monospace element */
  .receipt {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, 'Cascadia Code', monospace;
  }
  .receipt-head {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    background: var(--bg-elevated);
  }
  .receipt-tag {
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .receipt-fields {
    padding: 1rem;
    background: var(--bg-main);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
    gap: 0.5rem 1rem;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
